<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-belarus">Brest-Litovsk</a>, signed on 3 March 1918 between Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, took Russia out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Russia gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, the Baltic lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transcaucasian-sejm">Transcaucasian Sejm</a> towards independence. The treaty was annulled after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in November 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk.jpg" alt="Treaty of Brest-Litovsk" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traktat_brzeski_1918.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In March 1918 the government moved the capital from Petrograd to Moscow and signed the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b> with the Central Powers, giving up Poland, the Baltic lands, Finland and Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b>, German and Austro-Hungarian forces advanced into the Donbas.</blockquote><a href="/state/donetsk-krivoy-rog-soviet-republic">Donetsk-Krivoy Rog Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b> in March 1918, Germany allowed client institutions, including the Lithuanian Taryba, which had declared independence in February 1918, and planned a Duchy of Courland and Semigallia (1918).</blockquote><a href="/state/ober-ost">Ober Ost</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b>, Austro-Hungarian and German troops advanced on Odessa, and the Soviet leaders evacuated the city in March 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/odessa-soviet-republic">Odessa Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Armistice of Mudros in October 1918, the Ottoman Empire had to withdraw from Kars, Ardahan and Batum, lands it had gained by the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/southwestern-caucasian-republic">Southwestern Caucasian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Under pressure from Ottoman advances and the <b>Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</b>, the Sejm declared independence in April 1918, replacing the Commissariat with the Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/transcaucasian-commissariat">Transcaucasian Commissariat</a></li></ul></section>
